WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.835
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.170
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.915
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.915 --> 00:00:07.790
CAROLYN JONS: This is
Goodie Bag number nine.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.590
In today's video,
we will be exploring

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.170
the topics of polymerization
and cross-linking

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:13.790
and learning how
these processes can

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.190
be used to make slime at home.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:19.430
You'll need gloves,
Elmer's glue, borax powder,

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.520
plastic cups, and
spoons for stirring.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.740
One product made of polymers
that many people are

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.600
familiar with is Silly Putty.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.330
Silly Putty is made of a polymer
called polydimethylsiloxane.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.630
Polydimethylsiloxane
is made of repeating

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.840
units called monomers that
include a silicon, an oxygen,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.720
and two CH3 groups.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.120
Today we're going to make
an alternative material that

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.860
behaves a little bit
like Silly Putty,

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.350
but is instead made of
a different polymer.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.020
This can be made by
combining Elmer's glue

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.535
and cross-linking with borax.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.660
As you can see, after I added
borax to the Elmer's glue,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.820
the material became
much more viscous.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.680
This is because
of cross-linking.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.410
Cross-linking is forming
a bond to link one polymer

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.600
chain to another.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.830
In this experiment,
the polyvinyl alcohol

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.730
and Elmer's glue forms hydrogen
bonds with the boric acid.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.220
These cross-links make it
more difficult for the polymer

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.865
chains to slide
past one another,

00:01:24.865 --> 00:01:27.360
so the material
becomes more viscous.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.700
By adding in more borax,
we increase the number

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.800
of cross-links, and this
results in a material

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.360
more resistant to flow.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.370
You may have noticed that
when we pull quickly,

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:37.600
the slime breaks in half.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.450
But when we pull slowly, the
slime is able to stretch.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.660
This is because the slow pulling
allows hydrogen bonds to break

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.480
and then reform, allowing the
chains to more easily slide

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.880
past one another.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.250
In this Goodie Bag, we
hope you had an opportunity

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.220
to make a polymer of your own
and witness how cross-linking

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.270
can change material properties.

